hoices 17592186113594] [:db/retract 17592186113592 :answer-field/choices 17592186113597] [:db/retract 17592186113767 :answer-field/choices 17592186113769] [:db/retract 17592186113767 :answer-field/choices 17592186113770] [:db/retract 17592186113767 :answer-field/choices 17592186113771] [:db/retract 17592186113767 :answer-field/choices 17592186113772] {:db/id 17592186115394 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0b1d012f-068b-5ef8-8288-effe2d51246e" "question-8698245e-3b42-575d-8a24-d13bd09126d2" "question-48e5a226-dec1-538c-a739-b2da6b892931" "question-dd43e1d4-a6c0-5d8f-9dc6-6b6b8e30eb50"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-05950ac2-f758-5682-b193-f12241b0a085" "question-1380f470-1cae-5a0e-b171-8e1423867447" "question-5dae5e77-95b3-5116-bdbb-e15146f075a1" "question-56eb1f68-e0b0-5d8c-82fe-65522b38bb34"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115396 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-22fc3cb9-9f8c-550e-b1a7-090b46545ee2" "question-d69b9419-ecda-5adb-b076-9a4d08de1698" "question-82aa3655-fc19-581e-ac6b-187bbf004ee0" "question-43d07e17-8355-5232-ab17-51baae273661"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115397 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cff5ef5d-ae0d-5005-af50-0efa2caca50a" "question-ef76f5ac-70f5-5f95-86dd-2e2b577b8988" "question-ef36aafd-d427-51c9-9cd0-af8bbd24e5b8" "question-48d56ec2-9a26-5561-ad14-109677da4c62"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0b1d012f-068b-5ef8-8288-effe2d51246e" :question/id #uuid "0b1d012f-068b-5ef8-8288-effe2d51246e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127810" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{aligned}]$  is continuous." :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded points $t=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{t}$ is continuous for $t\\ge 0.$\n\n- The function $t+3$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for all $t\\ge 0$ except the point $t=1.$ So the interval of continuity is $[0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1aec6766-90e2-5088-80c3-02f015d74372"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1aec6766-90e2-5088-80c3-02f015d74372" :answer-field/id #uuid "1aec6766-90e2-5088-80c3-02f015d74372" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1c2e53bc-1e51-5158-be37-150f2d6da11f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-de7f6515-a2ae-5122-8741-2cd8e6ab4ba8" "answer-4a83e165-b269-52f5-a197-caf273f7ea71" "answer-e0ae7443-43d6-5655-96bf-fbf2d09a5527" "answer-1c2e53bc-1e51-5158-be37-150f2d6da11f" "answer-5010e8d0-e598-5d84-91f0-984ce80e6324"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de7f6515-a2ae-5122-8741-2cd8e6ab4ba8" :answer/id #uuid "de7f6515-a2ae-5122-8741-2cd8e6ab4ba8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a83e165-b269-52f5-a197-caf273f7ea71" :answer/id #uuid "4a83e165-b269-52f5-a197-caf273f7ea71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0ae7443-43d6-5655-96bf-fbf2d09a5527" :answer/id #uuid "e0ae7443-43d6-5655-96bf-fbf2d09a5527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c2e53bc-1e51-5158-be37-150f2d6da11f" :answer/id #uuid "1c2e53bc-1e51-5158-be37-150f2d6da11f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5010e8d0-e598-5d84-91f0-984ce80e6324" :answer/id #uuid "5010e8d0-e598-5d84-91f0-984ce80e6324" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8698245e-3b42-575d-8a24-d13bd09126d2" :question/id #uuid "8698245e-3b42-575d-8a24-d13bd09126d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-70558" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,k$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one-by-one.\n\n- The function $\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}$ is continuous everywhere except the excluded point $t=0.$\n\n- The function ${t}^{2}-1$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\frac{1}{t-2}$ is continuous everywhere except the point $t=2.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous everywhere except the points $t=0$ and $t=2.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a730784-58ce-5e73-830c-2d3bc30ec8f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a730784-58ce-5e73-830c-2d3bc30ec8f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a730784-58ce-5e73-830c-2d3bc30ec8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5aef3009-5a2d-508d-adca-eeca99616f2f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a568051a-98f8-5b96-85bc-e0f25ae0b93c" "answer-82573932-d8c9-5582-98a9-f1ce734bb0ed" "answer-5aef3009-5a2d-508d-adca-eeca99616f2f" "answer-3eca838d-2fc1-5d4f-aaa8-60a40708145b" "answer-18d1dd38-790c-5ade-b6e6-f0c5c753a1ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a568051a-98f8-5b96-85bc-e0f25ae0b93c" :answer/id #uuid "a568051a-98f8-5b96-85bc-e0f25ae0b93c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82573932-d8c9-5582-98a9-f1ce734bb0ed" :answer/id #uuid "82573932-d8c9-5582-98a9-f1ce734bb0ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aef3009-5a2d-508d-adca-eeca99616f2f" :answer/id #uuid "5aef3009-5a2d-508d-adca-eeca99616f2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3eca838d-2fc1-5d4f-aaa8-60a40708145b" :answer/id #uuid "3eca838d-2fc1-5d4f-aaa8-60a40708145b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18d1dd38-790c-5ade-b6e6-f0c5c753a1ee" :answer/id #uuid "18d1dd38-790c-5ade-b6e6-f0c5c753a1ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-48e5a226-dec1-538c-a739-b2da6b892931" :question/id #uuid "48e5a226-dec1-538c-a739-b2da6b892931" :question/math-academy-id "q-127807" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous on $[-1,1]?$\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(s) & =|{s}^{2}-1|\\,i+\\frac{s+1}{s}\\,j+2{s}^{2}\\,k \\\\ q(s) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)\\,i+({s}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\,k \\\\ r(s) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\,i-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡s\\,j+\\operatorname{csc}⁡s\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.  \n\nLet's consider the functions one by one. \n\n- The function $p(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits second component, $\\frac{s+1}{s},$ is undefined at $s=0$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function $q(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits first component, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-1)$, is undefined at $s=1$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function $r(s)$ is **not** continuous on $[-1,1]$ because \nits third component, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡s$, is undefined at $s=0$ and hence discontinuous on $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions are continuous on $[-1,1].$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e733f8a2-e030-5a6d-ae1a-9d5ff92e104f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e733f8a2-e030-5a6d-ae1a-9d5ff92e104f" :answer-field/id #uuid "e733f8a2-e030-5a6d-ae1a-9d5ff92e104f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-897e666e-5017-5c68-b94e-f2a61cddac86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98a35e8c-50cb-52af-93af-e4c92463935b" "answer-8d44025a-769b-53ed-8380-a28de619549a" "answer-5f216b60-e388-51f5-ba21-e8378f9d2558" "answer-ea628c3b-5f80-5519-9722-358822a7671c" "answer-897e666e-5017-5c68-b94e-f2a61cddac86"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98a35e8c-50cb-52af-93af-e4c92463935b" :answer/id #uuid "98a35e8c-50cb-52af-93af-e4c92463935b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r(s)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d44025a-769b-53ed-8380-a28de619549a" :answer/id #uuid "8d44025a-769b-53ed-8380-a28de619549a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$p(s)$  and $q(s)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f216b60-e388-51f5-ba21-e8378f9d2558" :answer/id #uuid "5f216b60-e388-51f5-ba21-e8378f9d2558" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$p(s)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea628c3b-5f80-5519-9722-358822a7671c" :answer/id #uuid "ea628c3b-5f80-5519-9722-358822a7671c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$q(s)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-897e666e-5017-5c68-b94e-f2a61cddac86" :answer/id #uuid "897e666e-5017-5c68-b94e-f2a61cddac86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the functions are continuous on $[-1,1]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115761 :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous at $t=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+(2t+1)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ h(t) & =2t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous at a point if and only if each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- Function $f(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ is not continuous at $t=0.$ Indeed, the left and right limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to +0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=+\\infty  \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to -0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ doesn’t exist.\n\n- Function $g(t)$ is **not** continuous at $t=0$ since its first component $\\frac{2}{t}$ is not continuous at $t=0.$\n\n- Function $h(t)$ is continuous at $t=0$ because each of its components $2t$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and ${t}^{4}$ are continuous at $t=0.$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $h(t)$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113532 :answer-field/correct 17592186113536 :answer-field/choices ["answer-c98a9e61-787a-5809-8c15-d98c6271b0d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c98a9e61-787a-5809-8c15-d98c6271b0d3" :answer/id #uuid "c98a9e61-787a-5809-8c15-d98c6271b0d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the functions are continuous at $t=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd43e1d4-a6c0-5d8f-9dc6-6b6b8e30eb50" :question/id #uuid "dd43e1d4-a6c0-5d8f-9dc6-6b6b8e30eb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-127809" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2{e}^{t-1},\\,\\sqrt{5-t}⟩$ is continuous." :question/worked-solution "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the components of $f(t)$ one by one.\n\n- The function $t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $2{e}^{t-1}$ is continuous for all real numbers.\n\n- The function $\\sqrt{5-t}$ is continuous for $t\\le 5.$\n\nTherefore, the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is continuous for $t\\le 5.$ So the interval of continuity is $(-\\infty ,5].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-32f815ec-32d1-5da6-a873-03e986d0bb8f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-32f815ec-32d1-5da6-a873-03e986d0bb8f" :answer-field/id #uuid "32f815ec-32d1-5da6-a873-03e986d0bb8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ebfed41d-7c24-5273-bbf1-93cf15a6a3ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f4f36e5-b04e-5bd5-a6fb-83da272ae33c" "answer-ebfed41d-7c24-5273-bbf1-93cf15a6a3ff" "answer-6982b8e4-6126-5d97-ba8a-51a2aac2d0ab" "answer-608e2ed1-b00d-5789-b147-2890b9a3778f" "answer-2992adf6-cddf-5fc4-9787-9ba131d5b5d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f4f36e5-b04e-5bd5-a6fb-83da272ae33c" :answer/id #uuid "0f4f36e5-b04e-5bd5-a6fb-83da272ae33c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-5]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebfed41d-7c24-5273-bbf1-93cf15a6a3ff" :answer/id #uuid "ebfed41d-7c24-5273-bbf1-93cf15a6a3ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,5]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6982b8e4-6126-5d97-ba8a-51a2aac2d0ab" :answer/id #uuid "6982b8e4-6126-5d97-ba8a-51a2aac2d0ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-608e2ed1-b00d-5789-b147-2890b9a3778f" :answer/id #uuid "608e2ed1-b00d-5789-b147-2890b9a3778f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-5,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2992adf6-cddf-5fc4-9787-9ba131d5b5d9" :answer/id #uuid "2992adf6-cddf-5fc4-9787-9ba131d5b5d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[5,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-05950ac2-f758-5682-b193-f12241b0a085" :question/id #uuid "05950ac2-f758-5682-b193-f12241b0a085" :question/math-academy-id "q-127820" :question/problem "Find $f(5)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(5)=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3{{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88941887-29dc-54a4-8201-4203e16bb84e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88941887-29dc-54a4-8201-4203e16bb84e" :answer-field/id #uuid "88941887-29dc-54a4-8201-4203e16bb84e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24d7bfc2-51e1-5033-adbd-48343a267821" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2918dce8-4f46-5431-aa05-6401af531bcf" "answer-caef0b1a-9245-5da8-b22a-8dda3c23c0a7" "answer-21d9e57e-bfa3-586e-9b91-d4dc21a2545d" "answer-04142752-c01a-5e11-89a6-6b5adc4ed5ae" "answer-24d7bfc2-51e1-5033-adbd-48343a267821"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2918dce8-4f46-5431-aa05-6401af531bcf" :answer/id #uuid "2918dce8-4f46-5431-aa05-6401af531bcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,0,3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caef0b1a-9245-5da8-b22a-8dda3c23c0a7" :answer/id #uuid "caef0b1a-9245-5da8-b22a-8dda3c23c0a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,1,6⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21d9e57e-bfa3-586e-9b91-d4dc21a2545d" :answer/id #uuid "21d9e57e-bfa3-586e-9b91-d4dc21a2545d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨4,-1,3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04142752-c01a-5e11-89a6-6b5adc4ed5ae" :answer/id #uuid "04142752-c01a-5e11-89a6-6b5adc4ed5ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨4,-2,4⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24d7bfc2-51e1-5033-adbd-48343a267821" :answer/id #uuid "24d7bfc2-51e1-5033-adbd-48343a267821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨4,0,7⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1380f470-1cae-5a0e-b171-8e1423867447" :question/id #uuid "1380f470-1cae-5a0e-b171-8e1423867447" :question/math-academy-id "q-70565" :question/problem "Given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(-7)=⟨6,0,-2⟩$ and $g(-7)=⟨2,-1,0⟩,$ determine\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to -7}\\frac{f(t)}{2}+{lim}_{t\\to -7}3g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to -7}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}f(-7)+3g(-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨6,0,-2⟩+3⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-1⟩+⟨6,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f54036d0-b672-540d-9df5-0a5f88f5edb4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f54036d0-b672-540d-9df5-0a5f88f5edb4" :answer-field/id #uuid "f54036d0-b672-540d-9df5-0a5f88f5edb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0f5e40e-4e6a-5985-b20a-99c944f7f9ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a8c966e9-c274-5eab-aa26-39cd5b33e082" "answer-30d9530a-4fda-58ce-be9d-a68a94510ffa" "answer-4befd6a6-4c2e-5f24-bda2-c235b60305c7" "answer-b472ddc0-a0fd-58d7-b0a3-868062a444c3" "answer-d0f5e40e-4e6a-5985-b20a-99c944f7f9ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8c966e9-c274-5eab-aa26-39cd5b33e082" :answer/id #uuid "a8c966e9-c274-5eab-aa26-39cd5b33e082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,2,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30d9530a-4fda-58ce-be9d-a68a94510ffa" :answer/id #uuid "30d9530a-4fda-58ce-be9d-a68a94510ffa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨6,-3,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4befd6a6-4c2e-5f24-bda2-c235b60305c7" :answer/id #uuid "4befd6a6-4c2e-5f24-bda2-c235b60305c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨6,2,-3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b472ddc0-a0fd-58d7-b0a3-868062a444c3" :answer/id #uuid "b472ddc0-a0fd-58d7-b0a3-868062a444c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨9,-1,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0f5e40e-4e6a-5985-b20a-99c944f7f9ea" :answer/id #uuid "d0f5e40e-4e6a-5985-b20a-99c944f7f9ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨9,-3,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5dae5e77-95b3-5116-bdbb-e15146f075a1" :question/id #uuid "5dae5e77-95b3-5116-bdbb-e15146f075a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71006" :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that $f(t)$ is a continuous vector-valued function and $u(t)$ is continuous real-valued function for which $u(0)=3$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}u(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) \\\\  & =u(0)f(0) \\\\  & =3f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ 3f(0) & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-6,-7,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-36617721-cdaf-5e1b-9dc1-a68d20a6f48d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-36617721-cdaf-5e1b-9dc1-a68d20a6f48d" :answer-field/id #uuid "36617721-cdaf-5e1b-9dc1-a68d20a6f48d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fae49f8d-3748-58b3-8819-760de320ce89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0456835a-ab1e-5d54-96b5-8385b0418d41" "answer-fae49f8d-3748-58b3-8819-760de320ce89" "answer-8400f582-fe6b-5605-9b73-4b03d4757ed8" "answer-8c32fdb6-a254-566c-8621-94a8ef887edb" "answer-23bce016-f53e-5fef-bcdf-fcb816c7238e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0456835a-ab1e-5d54-96b5-8385b0418d41" :answer/id #uuid "0456835a-ab1e-5d54-96b5-8385b0418d41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-6,-21,6⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fae49f8d-3748-58b3-8819-760de320ce89" :answer/id #uuid "fae49f8d-3748-58b3-8819-760de320ce89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-6,-7,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8400f582-fe6b-5605-9b73-4b03d4757ed8" :answer/id #uuid "8400f582-fe6b-5605-9b73-4b03d4757ed8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-9,-3,6⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c32fdb6-a254-566c-8621-94a8ef887edb" :answer/id #uuid "8c32fdb6-a254-566c-8621-94a8ef887edb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨5,-4,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23bce016-f53e-5fef-bcdf-fcb816c7238e" :answer/id #uuid "23bce016-f53e-5fef-bcdf-fcb816c7238e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨6,21,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115804 :question/problem "Find $g(2)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(2)=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple, and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 2}2g(t) \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =f(2)+2g(2) \\\\  & ={{⟨1,1,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(2)}+2g(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,2⟩+2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =⟨-1,3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113761 :answer-field/correct "answer-b02a670b-dc6a-56a6-bfc7-88e12bb41e1c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b02a670b-dc6a-56a6-bfc7-88e12bb41e1c" "answer-63cd7361-80a7-5610-b411-db6d96b03f98" "answer-8b1fb0b6-ee0d-51a1-80d9-cbcb788fb076" "answer-db46a840-c91e-50d2-8618-cbba1038d021" "answer-54aee2c5-cc51-57ec-9a8a-f399690213ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b02a670b-dc6a-56a6-bfc7-88e12bb41e1c" :answer/id #uuid "b02a670b-dc6a-56a6-bfc7-88e12bb41e1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,3,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63cd7361-80a7-5610-b411-db6d96b03f98" :answer/id #uuid "63cd7361-80a7-5610-b411-db6d96b03f98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-2,6,-2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b1fb0b6-ee0d-51a1-80d9-cbcb788fb076" :answer/id #uuid "8b1fb0b6-ee0d-51a1-80d9-cbcb788fb076" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,4,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db46a840-c91e-50d2-8618-cbba1038d021" :answer/id #uuid "db46a840-c91e-50d2-8618-cbba1038d021" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,8,4⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54aee2c5-cc51-57ec-9a8a-f399690213ae" :answer/id #uuid "54aee2c5-cc51-57ec-9a8a-f399690213ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-56eb1f68-e0b0-5d8c-82fe-65522b38bb34" :question/id #uuid "56eb1f68-e0b0-5d8c-82fe-65522b38bb34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35115" :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(0)=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-{{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9fe35960-523d-5648-9c8e-2980e2ae45c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9fe35960-523d-5648-9c8e-2980e2ae45c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fe35960-523d-5648-9c8e-2980e2ae45c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f107cc53-ee96-570c-9ee3-a5f4ee8def54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44b00ccb-8b01-596c-b4dc-2a3f3aa5a049" "answer-f107cc53-ee96-570c-9ee3-a5f4ee8def54" "answer-8ea82692-100a-5038-b8d4-644562b66a89" "answer-ce1f037b-158b-5c79-8e25-3276bfa998c9" "answer-45c07133-7c78-580f-9ccc-4af3dc31a4d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44b00ccb-8b01-596c-b4dc-2a3f3aa5a049" :answer/id #uuid "44b00ccb-8b01-596c-b4dc-2a3f3aa5a049" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,-3,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f107cc53-ee96-570c-9ee3-a5f4ee8def54" :answer/id #uuid "f107cc53-ee96-570c-9ee3-a5f4ee8def54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-3,-3,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ea82692-100a-5038-b8d4-644562b66a89" :answer/id #uuid "8ea82692-100a-5038-b8d4-644562b66a89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-3,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce1f037b-158b-5c79-8e25-3276bfa998c9" :answer/id #uuid "ce1f037b-158b-5c79-8e25-3276bfa998c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,3,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45c07133-7c78-580f-9ccc-4af3dc31a4d4" :answer/id #uuid "45c07133-7c78-580f-9ccc-4af3dc31a4d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The value of $f(0)$ cannot be determined." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22fc3cb9-9f8c-550e-b1a7-090b46545ee2" :question/id #uuid "22fc3cb9-9f8c-550e-b1a7-090b46545ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-70566" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{t-4}\\,j-5{t}^{2}\\,k$ is differentiable." :question/worked-solution "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one-by-one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{t}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{t})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=0,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{t}$ is not differentiable at this point.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{t-4})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{(t-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\frac{1}{t-4}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $-5{t}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{(-5{t}^{2})}^{′}=-10t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-5{t}^{2}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ),$ except the point $t=0,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f7ff639-4ca5-57e6-a7d3-21f3b2dfb18a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f7ff639-4ca5-57e6-a7d3-21f3b2dfb18a" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f7ff639-4ca5-57e6-a7d3-21f3b2dfb18a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-29f5c5e8-ff02-5056-940b-e508ad765aa6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f50708b8-5d3c-5122-8662-e4f8a58bf6f6" "answer-541e6159-1300-54d2-9ca7-82d337be42a8" "answer-29f5c5e8-ff02-5056-940b-e508ad765aa6" "answer-07fa6bb7-d0f9-5e18-9b3c-e212aeb79600" "answer-96a3c878-7b87-5ad8-a727-56d45e5d6afd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f50708b8-5d3c-5122-8662-e4f8a58bf6f6" :answer/id #uuid "f50708b8-5d3c-5122-8662-e4f8a58bf6f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-541e6159-1300-54d2-9ca7-82d337be42a8" :answer/id #uuid "541e6159-1300-54d2-9ca7-82d337be42a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29f5c5e8-ff02-5056-940b-e508ad765aa6" :answer/id #uuid "29f5c5e8-ff02-5056-940b-e508ad765aa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07fa6bb7-d0f9-5e18-9b3c-e212aeb79600" :answer/id #uuid "07fa6bb7-d0f9-5e18-9b3c-e212aeb79600" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96a3c878-7b87-5ad8-a727-56d45e5d6afd" :answer/id #uuid "96a3c878-7b87-5ad8-a727-56d45e5d6afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,4)\\cup (4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d69b9419-ecda-5adb-b076-9a4d08de1698" :question/id #uuid "d69b9419-ecda-5adb-b076-9a4d08de1698" :question/math-academy-id "q-32701" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨t\\sqrt{t},\\,1-{t}^{2},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$ if and only if each of its component functions is differentiable on that interval. \n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$ to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{1/2},\\,-2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t},\\,-2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of  ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we have that $g(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of  ${h}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $t\\in (\\pi ,2\\pi )$, we conclude that $h(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is: \n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-24c4782e-d988-5b6a-b328-68cc3dd0adb4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-24c4782e-d988-5b6a-b328-68cc3dd0adb4" :answer-field/id #uuid "24c4782e-d988-5b6a-b328-68cc3dd0adb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1662f69d-0d6b-5863-8f09-5d9d05419260" :answer-field/choices ["answer-014d7fc8-5099-5882-8aa1-ded2fbbcf5aa" "answer-5fdede5d-fa26-5289-a238-94143dcae14e" "answer-9706168f-6882-59ce-be20-eefcba9f53d5" "answer-1662f69d-0d6b-5863-8f09-5d9d05419260" "answer-dab073b4-14ff-58ad-bfaa-7282357cb131"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-014d7fc8-5099-5882-8aa1-ded2fbbcf5aa" :answer/id #uuid "014d7fc8-5099-5882-8aa1-ded2fbbcf5aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fdede5d-fa26-5289-a238-94143dcae14e" :answer/id #uuid "5fdede5d-fa26-5289-a238-94143dcae14e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9706168f-6882-59ce-be20-eefcba9f53d5" :answer/id #uuid "9706168f-6882-59ce-be20-eefcba9f53d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1662f69d-0d6b-5863-8f09-5d9d05419260" :answer/id #uuid "1662f69d-0d6b-5863-8f09-5d9d05419260" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dab073b4-14ff-58ad-bfaa-7282357cb131" :answer/id #uuid "dab073b4-14ff-58ad-bfaa-7282357cb131" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the functions are differentiable on $(\\pi ,2\\pi )$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-82aa3655-fc19-581e-ac6b-187bbf004ee0" :question/id #uuid "82aa3655-fc19-581e-ac6b-187bbf004ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-70480" :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in [0,1]?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =⟨t-1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ q(t) & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\sqrt[3]{2t-1}⟩ \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3t+2,\\,|t|,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in [0,1].$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in [0,1]$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one by one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $[0,1],$ we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{2t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{(2t-1)^{2}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the third component of this vector, $\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt[3]{(2t-1)^{2}}},$ is undefined at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that the function $q(t)$ is not differentiable on $[0,1].$\n\n- Finally, since the deviative of the second component $|t|$ of $r(t)$ is undefined at $t=0,$ we conclude that $r(t)$ is not differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"$p(t)$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-19f0265f-4630-5691-88b4-b34a3b810e7b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19f0265f-4630-5691-88b4-b34a3b810e7b" :answer-field/id #uuid "19f0265f-4630-5691-88b4-b34a3b810e7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea9caceb-1164-5695-8a9d-1fecd205232e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f8bf877f-6b9d-5bf9-96b6-7245c5fd452f" "answer-2a9e5bb1-dd1e-56cc-a986-5db0a68be56d" "answer-ea9caceb-1164-5695-8a9d-1fecd205232e" "answer-e0688584-1da5-5b8b-8b3f-e24c2240ab4c" "answer-ed0f2c96-0b6d-51f6-9a90-39072da93530"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8bf877f-6b9d-5bf9-96b6-7245c5fd452f" :answer/id #uuid "f8bf877f-6b9d-5bf9-96b6-7245c5fd452f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$p(t)$ and $q(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a9e5bb1-dd1e-56cc-a986-5db0a68be56d" :answer/id #uuid "2a9e5bb1-dd1e-56cc-a986-5db0a68be56d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$p(t)$ and $r(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea9caceb-1164-5695-8a9d-1fecd205232e" :answer/id #uuid "ea9caceb-1164-5695-8a9d-1fecd205232e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$p(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0688584-1da5-5b8b-8b3f-e24c2240ab4c" :answer/id #uuid "e0688584-1da5-5b8b-8b3f-e24c2240ab4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$q(t)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed0f2c96-0b6d-51f6-9a90-39072da93530" :answer/id #uuid "ed0f2c96-0b6d-51f6-9a90-39072da93530" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the functions are differentiable on $t\\in [0,1]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-43d07e17-8355-5232-ab17-51baae273661" :question/id #uuid "43d07e17-8355-5232-ab17-51baae273661" :question/math-academy-id "q-127855" :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ is differentiable." :question/worked-solution "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one by one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}^{′}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1})}^{′}=\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}$ is not differentiable at this points.\n\n- The derivative of  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is\n\n$$\n{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ we conclude that  $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ except the points $t=\\pm 1,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c4af1a7d-f52e-5d64-bf12-4976f28c7f6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c4af1a7d-f52e-5d64-bf12-4976f28c7f6d" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4af1a7d-f52e-5d64-bf12-4976f28c7f6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ab6c84d-a691-5a1a-acd5-3b2973b35c94" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1681cfe9-8bfe-557b-bba9-dee04b551092" "answer-b1e97209-1671-561b-bcf4-837548575394" "answer-2ab6c84d-a691-5a1a-acd5-3b2973b35c94" "answer-e6dc9c5c-0a08-5283-9a8c-3162c5f31765" "answer-92fe21ff-aedd-582d-862d-28a80840405d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1681cfe9-8bfe-557b-bba9-dee04b551092" :answer/id #uuid "1681cfe9-8bfe-557b-bba9-dee04b551092" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1e97209-1671-561b-bcf4-837548575394" :answer/id #uuid "b1e97209-1671-561b-bcf4-837548575394" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ab6c84d-a691-5a1a-acd5-3b2973b35c94" :answer/id #uuid "2ab6c84d-a691-5a1a-acd5-3b2973b35c94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6dc9c5c-0a08-5283-9a8c-3162c5f31765" :answer/id #uuid "e6dc9c5c-0a08-5283-9a8c-3162c5f31765" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92fe21ff-aedd-582d-862d-28a80840405d" :answer/id #uuid "92fe21ff-aedd-582d-862d-28a80840405d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115771 :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (0,1)?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =⟨2t-1,\\,{t}^{2}+4,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ q(t) & =⟨(t+1)^{4},\\,\\sqrt[3]{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ \\\\ r(t) & =⟨3t+2,\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined on $t\\in (0,1).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in (0,1)$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(t+1)^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${q}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we have that $q(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $r(t)$ to find ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${r}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $r(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113592 :answer-field/correct "answer-9f0eae6e-708e-5574-aa53-37eb5b37b2c4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f0eae6e-708e-5574-aa53-37eb5b37b2c4" "answer-2bc1a731-b484-519e-93a3-569ae5979606"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f0eae6e-708e-5574-aa53-37eb5b37b2c4" :answer/id #uuid "9f0eae6e-708e-5574-aa53-37eb5b37b2c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the functions are differentiable on $t\\in (0,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bc1a731-b484-519e-93a3-569ae5979606" :answer/id #uuid "2bc1a731-b484-519e-93a3-569ae5979606" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the functions are differentiable on $t\\in (0,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cff5ef5d-ae0d-5005-af50-0efa2caca50a" :question/id #uuid "cff5ef5d-ae0d-5005-af50-0efa2caca50a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71094" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={t}^{2}+2 \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt[3]{t-3}\\,i+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where both $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. The vector function $g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where its components are differentiable.  \n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. \n\nFor the real-valued function $u(t):$  \n\n- We observe that $u(t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component, $\\sqrt[3]{t-3}$, is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$ as its derivative is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second and third components, ${cos}^{2}⁡t$ and $\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}},$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the domains of all three components of  $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$  \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9188640e-0ced-51d7-bb85-9890813bb8e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9188640e-0ced-51d7-bb85-9890813bb8e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "9188640e-0ced-51d7-bb85-9890813bb8e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34dd1b4d-1762-57e5-a082-1e6af4b9595a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3f808e0-fb9c-5164-9715-84ca77aa6cd0" "answer-d78598a0-c8b4-585e-b759-394d69f24982" "answer-93f606fe-6d18-58e7-bbf9-e3cdd3fe5e8f" "answer-34dd1b4d-1762-57e5-a082-1e6af4b9595a" "answer-e7a768fd-bbcd-5923-b417-ba559141df95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3f808e0-fb9c-5164-9715-84ca77aa6cd0" :answer/id #uuid "c3f808e0-fb9c-5164-9715-84ca77aa6cd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d78598a0-c8b4-585e-b759-394d69f24982" :answer/id #uuid "d78598a0-c8b4-585e-b759-394d69f24982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93f606fe-6d18-58e7-bbf9-e3cdd3fe5e8f" :answer/id #uuid "93f606fe-6d18-58e7-bbf9-e3cdd3fe5e8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34dd1b4d-1762-57e5-a082-1e6af4b9595a" :answer/id #uuid "34dd1b4d-1762-57e5-a082-1e6af4b9595a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7a768fd-bbcd-5923-b417-ba559141df95" :answer/id #uuid "e7a768fd-bbcd-5923-b417-ba559141df95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef76f5ac-70f5-5f95-86dd-2e2b577b8988" :question/id #uuid "ef76f5ac-70f5-5f95-86dd-2e2b577b8988" :question/math-academy-id "q-127893" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =|t-1{|}^{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{\\sqrt{t-1}}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $|t-1{|}^{3}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)$ is differentiable over $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(1,\\infty ).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable over $(-2,2).$\n\n- The second component ${e}^{\\sqrt{t-1}}$ is differentiable for $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{3}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(1,2).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is\n\n$$\n(1,\\infty )\\cap (1,2)=(1,2).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-816ea349-9124-5703-a18d-858815a67291"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-816ea349-9124-5703-a18d-858815a67291" :answer-field/id #uuid "816ea349-9124-5703-a18d-858815a67291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d31ed91-ba1f-5fb8-8b20-4a0b4f4638c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d272829-2e53-5971-995f-8a13d9832bf1" "answer-d39d0f66-0fea-55a8-95f1-96a73e042d6d" "answer-6d31ed91-ba1f-5fb8-8b20-4a0b4f4638c7" "answer-e1247620-39f5-51a2-a437-2fe0f4af181d" "answer-ff8f8ab7-5e9f-5a65-8f25-9e590ae8ecba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d272829-2e53-5971-995f-8a13d9832bf1" :answer/id #uuid "3d272829-2e53-5971-995f-8a13d9832bf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d39d0f66-0fea-55a8-95f1-96a73e042d6d" :answer/id #uuid "d39d0f66-0fea-55a8-95f1-96a73e042d6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d31ed91-ba1f-5fb8-8b20-4a0b4f4638c7" :answer/id #uuid "6d31ed91-ba1f-5fb8-8b20-4a0b4f4638c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1247620-39f5-51a2-a437-2fe0f4af181d" :answer/id #uuid "e1247620-39f5-51a2-a437-2fe0f4af181d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff8f8ab7-5e9f-5a65-8f25-9e590ae8ecba" :answer/id #uuid "ff8f8ab7-5e9f-5a65-8f25-9e590ae8ecba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef36aafd-d427-51c9-9cd0-af8bbd24e5b8" :question/id #uuid "ef36aafd-d427-51c9-9cd0-af8bbd24e5b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-71089" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\sqrt[3]{t}\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j+(5+t)\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt[3]{t}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at  $t=0.$\n\n- The second component ${e}^{2t}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $(5+t)$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is differentiable on $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is \n\n$$\n[(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )]\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89f9c708-4753-5381-8ffa-d3d47ae26eb9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89f9c708-4753-5381-8ffa-d3d47ae26eb9" :answer-field/id #uuid "89f9c708-4753-5381-8ffa-d3d47ae26eb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0acff39c-0666-5bce-aa8a-12219d111a79" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0acff39c-0666-5bce-aa8a-12219d111a79" "answer-a9b0a692-d075-5ddc-a635-2f86c53a60c3" "answer-5d1e8518-dddd-5a84-83ff-fecff1302c24" "answer-2e4d0662-0e86-5867-95df-e87c9d2e0aa4" "answer-062e3f00-c0cc-5c86-9d40-9b8fb8f5c06b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0acff39c-0666-5bce-aa8a-12219d111a79" :answer/id #uuid "0acff39c-0666-5bce-aa8a-12219d111a79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9b0a692-d075-5ddc-a635-2f86c53a60c3" :answer/id #uuid "a9b0a692-d075-5ddc-a635-2f86c53a60c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d1e8518-dddd-5a84-83ff-fecff1302c24" :answer/id #uuid "5d1e8518-dddd-5a84-83ff-fecff1302c24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e4d0662-0e86-5867-95df-e87c9d2e0aa4" :answer/id #uuid "2e4d0662-0e86-5867-95df-e87c9d2e0aa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-062e3f00-c0cc-5c86-9d40-9b8fb8f5c06b" :answer/id #uuid "062e3f00-c0cc-5c86-9d40-9b8fb8f5c06b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-48d56ec2-9a26-5561-ad14-109677da4c62" :question/id #uuid "48d56ec2-9a26-5561-ad14-109677da4c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-71088" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{t-3}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{3-t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{16-{t}^{2}}\\,i+{2}^{t-2}\\,j+({t}^{2}-4)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\frac{1}{t-3}$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )$, as it  is not defined at $t=3.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{3-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,3)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=3$ and the function is not defined for $t>3.$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,3).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{16-{t}^{2}}$ is differentiable on $(-4,4)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=\\pm 4$ and the function is not defined for $|t|>4.$\n\n- The second component ${2}^{t-2}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component ${t}^{2}-4$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-4,4).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cap (-4,4)=(-4,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6735ac2c-1ab2-5f2e-8ebd-5ac1244d08a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6735ac2c-1ab2-5f2e-8ebd-5ac1244d08a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "6735ac2c-1ab2-5f2e-8ebd-5ac1244d08a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ac377b35-816b-51bb-a07b-33e58ac30d79" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac377b35-816b-51bb-a07b-33e58ac30d79" "answer-4f341999-9ba5-5f4a-9140-656c713efee3" "answer-6ae917e7-8245-59fd-980b-c3a5c657ebed" "answer-f93684b1-e617-524d-a9bc-88188148d840" "answer-093035b3-2936-55d6-8dab-3391d410df31"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac377b35-816b-51bb-a07b-33e58ac30d79" :answer/id #uuid "ac377b35-816b-51bb-a07b-33e58ac30d79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f341999-9ba5-5f4a-9140-656c713efee3" :answer/id #uuid "4f341999-9ba5-5f4a-9140-656c713efee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ae917e7-8245-59fd-980b-c3a5c657ebed" :answer/id #uuid "6ae917e7-8245-59fd-980b-c3a5c657ebed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-4]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f93684b1-e617-524d-a9bc-88188148d840" :answer/id #uuid "f93684b1-e617-524d-a9bc-88188148d840" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-093035b3-2936-55d6-8dab-3391d410df31" :answer/id #uuid "093035b3-2936-55d6-8dab-3391d410df31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-4,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115805 :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable, given that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =|t-1|\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{1-t}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & ={t}^{2}\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\times g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $|t-1|$ is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=1.$\n\n- The second component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\sqrt{1-t}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=1$ and the function is not defined for $t>1.$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,1).$ \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component ${t}^{2}$ is differentiable for all values of $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component $\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-9}$ is differentiable for $(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)$ is differentiable only on $(-\\infty ,3).$\n\nThe intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,-3).$ \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for both functions is $(-\\infty ,1)\\cap (-\\infty ,-3)=(-\\infty ,-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113767 :answer-field/correct "answer-9f18fad1-1cd0-5b00-bdc0-9f0c14dfa40b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa5c267b-63e8-5dd0-bb3a-f4eac717c321" "answer-9f18fad1-1cd0-5b00-bdc0-9f0c14dfa40b" "answer-eafc1913-afb8-5c4d-969b-a557a69f332f" "answer-c838c1be-6f79-573c-844f-4ea6d6563720"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa5c267b-63e8-5dd0-bb3a-f4eac717c321" :answer/id #uuid "aa5c267b-63e8-5dd0-bb3a-f4eac717c321" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f18fad1-1cd0-5b00-bdc0-9f0c14dfa40b" :answer/id #uuid "9f18fad1-1cd0-5b00-bdc0-9f0c14dfa40b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eafc1913-afb8-5c4d-969b-a557a69f332f" :answer/id #uuid "eafc1913-afb8-5c4d-969b-a557a69f332f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c838c1be-6f79-573c-844f-4ea6d6563720" :answer/id #uuid "c838c1be-6f79-573c-844f-4ea6d6563720" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116443 :tutorial/content "Determining the continuity of a vector function is very similar to determining the continuity of a scalar function.  A vector function $\\mathbf f (t)$ is **continuous** at a point $t_0$ (in its domain) if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left \\langle e^t,  \\dfrac{1}{t} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis continuous at $t=1$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 1}({e}^{t}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit of a vector function is determined by the limits of the components, a vector function is continuous at a point $t_0$ *if and only if* each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nWe say that a vector function is **continuous on the interval** $t\\in[a,b]$ if it is continuous at every point of this interval." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116444 :tutorial/content "Similar to scalar-valued functions, the vector function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is **differentiable** at a point $t = t_0$ in the domain of the function if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\mathbf{f}(t_0+h) - \\mathbf{f}(t_0)}{h}\n$$\n\nThe value of the limit is then called the **derivative** of $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ at $t = t_0$ and is denoted as $\\mathbf{f}'(t_0)$. In general, a vector function is differentiable *if and only if* all of its components are differentiable.\n\nFor example, is the following vector function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ differentiable at $t=0$?\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{g}(t) = \\big\\langle t, \\: |\\,t\\,|, \\: \\sin t \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nNotice that the second component, $|\\,t\\,|,$ is not differentiable at $t=0.$ Since one of the component functions is not differentiable at $t=0,$ we conclude that the overall vector function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is not differentiable at $t=0.$ \n\nWe say that a vector function is **differentiable on an open interval** $t \\in (a,b)$ if it is differentiable at *every* point $t \\in (a,b).$ This means that each component of the function must be differentiable at every point of $(a,b).$\n\nOur function $\\mathbf{g}(t)$, mentioned above, is differentiable on $(-\\infty, 0) \\cup (0, \\infty)$ since all of its components are differentiable there.\n\nFinally, a function is said to be **continuously differentiable** if it is differentiable and its derivative is continuous." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115484 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115282 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a981d1c6-7401-5c05-a9ed-dfe4b1119a94"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115105 :question/problem "Plot the curve of $y=\\log_7 (x+4).$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\log_7 {(x+4)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\log_7 {x}.$\n\n![](images/97/975f58b320e2293dbfaf267a3acfe9187438ae40add3603bac98d142b35c44c1.png)\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\log_7 {(x+4)}.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce5db8eb1adb7e84337427c1c769eabec9cb420b51a7e867f47e8d573c697a79.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115325 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-67b6ab53-2529-5d71-90b1-cd110fcf91e4" "question-3efa74bc-65a6-5cab-855c-4b517911be86"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115148 :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd74c5b1a7233be5978a128a74956fa953cd78a737b8f40f68c1e42993f58140.png)\n\nGiven the diagram above, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=k\\log_2 {x}$ can be obtained from $y=\\log_2 {x}$ by a vertical stretch of scale factor $k.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(4,2)$ that lies on $y=\\log_2 {x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n(4,k \\cdot 2)=\\left(4,3\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=k\\log_2 {x}.$ \n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac 32.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115324 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1a4b2537-96ba-591d-822a-13598207943c" "question-416b8a87-9deb-532d-86eb-39a4fa9daecb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115147 :question/problem "Plot the graph of ![](images/54/54999da9704e7c5f8b341e30bb7af0d8c5c89b4cd91fa8be96d71521c30a6b95.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To plot ![](images/ea/ea6a0209e0fa8abfa9adbd2a10c238e598d2df19b3039eed6f9fef358c03f0b0.svg) we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve ![](images/4b/4bd2e8b39608d2ffef63de1d65e9fab3781acd8b8ea582a3a4d8357af49b1a77.svg)\n\n![](images/45/452610c472df847bb7a09815444c3d4f318008a8881864f668f153bee6cbae33.png)\n\n- Translate it by ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the right to get the graph of ![](images/c0/c0b21b4821b92baca78e191dad50f9f3a63cf0ddd8425c966ef5a2375a78262d.svg)\n\n![](images/5b/5bf8eba8adaa2e7221d77eca13bef8ee33a37ed05af2071e12fec12f4edb672d.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) parallel to the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis to get the graph of ![](images/ce/ce51c3344030b46eb0a3b4ed74f85e6284e4357a375c338035993147ee92f9fa.svg)\n\n![](images/90/90bd455c3a6b833b169c3207c58cc422c3fb963c7de9ae5f11bc0b6464117d6f.png)\n\n- Finally, translate the result by ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) unit up to get the graph of ![](images/54/54999da9704e7c5f8b341e30bb7af0d8c5c89b4cd91fa8be96d71521c30a6b95.svg)\n\n![](images/ad/ade4b0bff8907d7284cedd15895e997013121f0f91f2b82a0314f363a81ad3a9.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115283 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-736b21cf-cbb6-5fc6-8626-c4e49709882d" "question-7ee0988d-03dd-5330-89a0-142478096881"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115106 :question/problem "The diagram below shows the graph of $y=k \\log_5 (bx).$ Find the value of $b\\cdot k.$\n\n![](images/36/3698fc49edb443517ed0ee2cfa81c586c428f402df7028a8ec4b47702d8a4fad.png)" :question/worked-solution "Clearly, $b \\neq 0.$ Substituting $x=\\dfrac1b$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{5}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{5}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence, \n\n$$\n(2,0)  = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{5}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10) & =3 \\\\ k{log}_{5}⁡5 & =3 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a981d1c6-7401-5c05-a9ed-dfe4b1119a94" :question/id #uuid "a981d1c6-7401-5c05-a9ed-dfe4b1119a94" :question/math-academy-id "q-28248" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/e5/e595bc1d2c4998c19d1e878bf04856cabb3eef94e665e527449ded66eded6ef1.png)\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$\n\n![](images/4b/4b5e2469ce9b19817f67b95a0ac0b402a3d5dec6db92b79f7c7e18331ef267af.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad712332-f26b-5fe6-85be-ff600d60418a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad712332-f26b-5fe6-85be-ff600d60418a" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad712332-f26b-5fe6-85be-ff600d60418a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-31fb838b-38b2-5ea1-a6c5-3c99557057ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3598f73-5ff2-50c1-b4e7-46331d453f34" "answer-9d13fcf6-57bd-5155-a0d1-0fd1d61508e5" "answer-31fb838b-38b2-5ea1-a6c5-3c99557057ad" "answer-3dc4589a-2522-5497-8812-d066a6032475" "answer-e236c589-b299-55e0-bcbf-1880ceecb833"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3598f73-5ff2-50c1-b4e7-46331d453f34" :answer/id #uuid "f3598f73-5ff2-50c1-b4e7-46331d453f34" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/00/002d25cb0ab4e4e378fe34955796b56ecbc05dde470a0ded5cb54ce86cf36049.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d13fcf6-57bd-5155-a0d1-0fd1d61508e5" :answer/id #uuid "9d13fcf6-57bd-5155-a0d1-0fd1d61508e5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/14/14a1d62e03b380aec6bb164e28935531845f1f46f98ea5103bbb54b60594cdc4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31fb838b-38b2-5ea1-a6c5-3c99557057ad" :answer/id #uuid "31fb838b-38b2-5ea1-a6c5-3c99557057ad" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4b/4b5e2469ce9b19817f67b95a0ac0b402a3d5dec6db92b79f7c7e18331ef267af.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dc4589a-2522-5497-8812-d066a6032475" :answer/id #uuid "3dc4589a-2522-5497-8812-d066a6032475" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/58/5836978b2b72feee5a8d0dca2f63c2cf416259e1a93f5023a9aa0912eecd456a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e236c589-b299-55e0-bcbf-1880ceecb833" :answer/id #uuid "e236c589-b299-55e0-bcbf-1880ceecb833" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6b/6ba3a4e8232a6a40cd8866e0461c8a5226279529b5fc87622fa82643108fff80.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115674 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9a/9aa292639642f664a81078a5cf916a7f7efef4f4b4a944a85c17bfc63b11beb8.png)\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7c2d23d12059c5fe56ce44757825e6c95517e842caeded02b734bbfdc5f7945.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186113010 :answer-field/correct 17592186113012 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-67b6ab53-2529-5d71-90b1-cd110fcf91e4" :question/id #uuid "67b6ab53-2529-5d71-90b1-cd110fcf91e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-7869" :question/problem "![](images/56/569d6855e7ebdbd7c2e040d7f0200e72e0df71f88991085e3224570587c33767.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ and the point ${A}^{′}$ with coordinates $(6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=(6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-98ef2340-2a64-5e76-8501-961e9be00321"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98ef2340-2a64-5e76-8501-961e9be00321" :answer-field/id #uuid "98ef2340-2a64-5e76-8501-961e9be00321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-92c4cf5a-5e3f-5c1f-a1c4-be34f16977fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b399da8-7125-56d3-91c5-c76971e45597" "answer-816e73c6-67e3-54cb-841a-bd3150e1f747" "answer-a25796f8-3bad-5125-8704-1ef508d19cdf" "answer-92c4cf5a-5e3f-5c1f-a1c4-be34f16977fb" "answer-a2bc685b-8218-5d63-afa0-3e0398dc7fe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b399da8-7125-56d3-91c5-c76971e45597" :answer/id #uuid "8b399da8-7125-56d3-91c5-c76971e45597" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-816e73c6-67e3-54cb-841a-bd3150e1f747" :answer/id #uuid "816e73c6-67e3-54cb-841a-bd3150e1f747" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a25796f8-3bad-5125-8704-1ef508d19cdf" :answer/id #uuid "a25796f8-3bad-5125-8704-1ef508d19cdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92c4cf5a-5e3f-5c1f-a1c4-be34f16977fb" :answer/id #uuid "92c4cf5a-5e3f-5c1f-a1c4-be34f16977fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2bc685b-8218-5d63-afa0-3e0398dc7fe7" :answer/id #uuid "a2bc685b-8218-5d63-afa0-3e0398dc7fe7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3efa74bc-65a6-5cab-855c-4b517911be86" :question/id #uuid "3efa74bc-65a6-5cab-855c-4b517911be86" :question/math-academy-id "q-7870" :question/problem "![](images/98/98b9df96585a5e7067f229ed85a2e60309fbc56865d9bdf61d98aed68ac70ac2.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ and the point ${A}^{′}$ with coordinates $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-26a0aa5e-477f-56a9-b2e2-1c62979c6a31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-26a0aa5e-477f-56a9-b2e2-1c62979c6a31" :answer-field/id #uuid "26a0aa5e-477f-56a9-b2e2-1c62979c6a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec347080-5383-5630-bfde-2eff2087a0d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ec347080-5383-5630-bfde-2eff2087a0d3" "answer-6167675c-e9af-5f3b-8b02-9d41b7ffb5ad" "answer-6094db19-089e-5350-8ea3-f89bfab0e6c2" "answer-3aabb100-1696-5ca1-860c-17c2b46c3d08" "answer-b50612f6-c9dc-55aa-a261-7f8631d54606"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec347080-5383-5630-bfde-2eff2087a0d3" :answer/id #uuid "ec347080-5383-5630-bfde-2eff2087a0d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6167675c-e9af-5f3b-8b02-9d41b7ffb5ad" :answer/id #uuid "6167675c-e9af-5f3b-8b02-9d41b7ffb5ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6094db19-089e-5350-8ea3-f89bfab0e6c2" :answer/id #uuid "6094db19-089e-5350-8ea3-f89bfab0e6c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3aabb100-1696-5ca1-860c-17c2b46c3d08" :answer/id #uuid "3aabb100-1696-5ca1-860c-17c2b46c3d08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b50612f6-c9dc-55aa-a261-7f8631d54606" :answer/id #uuid "b50612f6-c9dc-55aa-a261-7f8631d54606" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1a4b2537-96ba-591d-822a-13598207943c" :question/id #uuid "1a4b2537-96ba-591d-822a-13598207943c" :question/math-academy-id "q-167914" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{log}_{3}⁡(x-2)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=4{log}_{3}⁡(x-2)-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y={log}_{3}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/76/76bd0cabc680c0f0ac7d21c2b1122bff3e05bd0b544a2043e9f8a6aa6056db06.png)\n\n- Translate it by $2$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡(x-2).$\n\n![](images/c3/c385f9b1786078fab7ee97b5b266454be8afe32dd3f82bfe2c35bc1ea1ff424d.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4{log}_{3}⁡(x-2).$\n\n![](images/a7/a7a1802a657832efcd1fd2ca8d2c34b9226b1b5324636f9779bda68b4f8646d1.png)\n\n- Finally, translate the result by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=4{log}_{3}⁡(x-2)-1.$\n\n![](images/34/3483850384b487ec9bb497cb328cf642fccf5605f9a766492d59082f3d0d5d12.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-115d8b1e-c643-5dda-a9bf-81845168e301"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-115d8b1e-c643-5dda-a9bf-81845168e301" :answer-field/id #uuid "115d8b1e-c643-5dda-a9bf-81845168e301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f4ae3510-0672-5760-a1ee-b1cf3da99f16" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4ae3510-0672-5760-a1ee-b1cf3da99f16" "answer-3854b07d-32e7-5537-be69-cc8b3ce59bd8" "answer-5246ac67-dbfb-5501-8b44-ffe95ae64c4a" "answer-4c121d05-33df-51a8-be1c-77a4c15bf74a" "answer-d704c739-42c2-55ac-ad13-b0d9b96eaa0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4ae3510-0672-5760-a1ee-b1cf3da99f16" :answer/id #uuid "f4ae3510-0672-5760-a1ee-b1cf3da99f16" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/34/3483850384b487ec9bb497cb328cf642fccf5605f9a766492d59082f3d0d5d12.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3854b07d-32e7-5537-be69-cc8b3ce59bd8" :answer/id #uuid "3854b07d-32e7-5537-be69-cc8b3ce59bd8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/48/48418c05467ca954bb2620ed356d1db9ce3c7774c5e41c6fa8b1f20cebc17bed.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5246ac67-dbfb-5501-8b44-ffe95ae64c4a" :answer/id #uuid "5246ac67-dbfb-5501-8b44-ffe95ae64c4a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a6/a6451cc35ed4d2a5b8241793e5ffeb165858f3e97c45461cf3259a61035ebabc.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c121d05-33df-51a8-be1c-77a4c15bf74a" :answer/id #uuid "4c121d05-33df-51a8-be1c-77a4c15bf74a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a7/a7a1802a657832efcd1fd2ca8d2c34b9226b1b5324636f9779bda68b4f8646d1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d704c739-42c2-55ac-ad13-b0d9b96eaa0a" :answer/id #uuid "d704c739-42c2-55ac-ad13-b0d9b96eaa0a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c6/c6924835aa443f57ad5044502fc2601d652b1f436709d0623e260a2ed40c80d9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-416b8a87-9deb-532d-86eb-39a4fa9daecb" :question/id #uuid "416b8a87-9deb-532d-86eb-39a4fa9daecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98092" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f12e7576bb07678189e775c8e2cc98f621e740b45c558c743423a707253c9f81.png)\n\n- Translate it by $3$ units to the left to get the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/e3/e313515347d6bebbaa76d047fd0900a5e712f64bed05c796907a54bf81628201.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3).$\n\n![](images/94/94ae95babe9a30030c18d67347b59999e3e25c201afe8aebf5ae4197c42138d6.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a264fcee-13bb-5baf-b833-1dc8f72ed71a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a264fcee-13bb-5baf-b833-1dc8f72ed71a" :answer-field/id #uuid "a264fcee-13bb-5baf-b833-1dc8f72ed71a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7bdcccdb-e57d-5f04-a7e6-7654ae8f2721" :answer-field/choices ["answer-655db017-19aa-5b27-bf3c-294f8cda967e" "answer-541193ee-865c-528d-a776-7703a6b15d27" "answer-11c9eccc-a983-5339-8a4d-2d4e3ca8e1df" "answer-7bdcccdb-e57d-5f04-a7e6-7654ae8f2721" "answer-152301c1-86ca-5115-ba98-91bad9da541b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-655db017-19aa-5b27-bf3c-294f8cda967e" :answer/id #uuid "655db017-19aa-5b27-bf3c-294f8cda967e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/45/45ad8d9d4e4ebf6b0d581ae4415dbbdad923622383d7b56278334a5eb151c0fe.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-541193ee-865c-528d-a776-7703a6b15d27" :answer/id #uuid "541193ee-865c-528d-a776-7703a6b15d27" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6a/6a94e8b714eb5d548a271e4ba5288c355b7b77a2e54d46795a2fe5c952741649.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11c9eccc-a983-5339-8a4d-2d4e3ca8e1df" :answer/id #uuid "11c9eccc-a983-5339-8a4d-2d4e3ca8e1df" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/81/81645f67d82542d23b38597a7bbb682028e8e59a8449f6c9d0beac96621d081b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bdcccdb-e57d-5f04-a7e6-7654ae8f2721" :answer/id #uuid "7bdcccdb-e57d-5f04-a7e6-7654ae8f2721" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/94/94ae95babe9a30030c18d67347b59999e3e25c201afe8aebf5ae4197c42138d6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-152301c1-86ca-5115-ba98-91bad9da541b" :answer/id #uuid "152301c1-86ca-5115-ba98-91bad9da541b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/9d/9dcd915da2122631cf6d7555668cd69ed15265e113acc99d9ad062ceee0090be.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-736b21cf-cbb6-5fc6-8626-c4e49709882d" :question/id #uuid "736b21cf-cbb6-5fc6-8626-c4e49709882d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98150" :question/problem "![](images/65/6583ad253785716c7ead3bb45083a6498fdf45b48c4572a7339b2e8c6f43266a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k{log}_{2}⁡(bx).$ Find the value of $k\\cdot b.$" :question/worked-solution "Clearly, $b\\ne 0.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{b}$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{2}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(\\frac{1}{b},0)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(3,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence\n\n$$\n(3,0)=(\\frac{1}{b},0)\\,⇒\\,b=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(6,2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{2}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6) & =2 \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡2 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nk\\cdot b=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27ae073a-5203-5077-b949-ae499f36c0ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27ae073a-5203-5077-b949-ae499f36c0ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "27ae073a-5203-5077-b949-ae499f36c0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c139792e-bada-5b41-886d-9250900c5b59" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac31a6ee-2e8d-52cd-b56a-12f4039e5b52" "answer-ab9acb21-3d7e-5418-97ba-19ff056536c8" "answer-75c99027-a990-59e8-84c8-d409c6f5f2c8" "answer-c139792e-bada-5b41-886d-9250900c5b59" "answer-8a753746-656b-5851-961e-7895d0368ce1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac31a6ee-2e8d-52cd-b56a-12f4039e5b52" :answer/id #uuid "ac31a6ee-2e8d-52cd-b56a-12f4039e5b52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab9acb21-3d7e-5418-97ba-19ff056536c8" :answer/id #uuid "ab9acb21-3d7e-5418-97ba-19ff056536c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75c99027-a990-59e8-84c8-d409c6f5f2c8" :answer/id #uuid "75c99027-a990-59e8-84c8-d409c6f5f2c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c139792e-bada-5b41-886d-9250900c5b59" :answer/id #uuid "c139792e-bada-5b41-886d-9250900c5b59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a753746-656b-5851-961e-7895d0368ce1" :answer/id #uuid "8a753746-656b-5851-961e-7895d0368ce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ee0988d-03dd-5330-89a0-142478096881" :question/id #uuid "7ee0988d-03dd-5330-89a0-142478096881" :question/math-academy-id "q-33465" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c09cf7f047cb341d3e2e2337ac41847d9cf5ff9435d075414a7db092aec3be2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y={log}_{4}⁡(x-d)+c.$ Find the value of $\\frac{d}{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "In the given graph, the vertical asymptote is $x=4.$ So, the argument of the logarithm function should evaluate to zero at $x=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n4-d=0\\,⇒\\,d=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(8,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(x-d)+c & =y \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(x-4)+c & =y \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(8-4)+c & =3 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(4)+c & =3 \\\\ 1+c & =3 \\\\ c & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{c}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0661678-c752-58dd-ae45-19a8a3ab5f43"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0661678-c752-58dd-ae45-19a8a3ab5f43" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0661678-c752-58dd-ae45-19a8a3ab5f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d728cb75-8aff-5441-a02b-d2123ed28dfa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3fb69761-90da-5ef3-9336-c74c1cd1883c" "answer-3035fbac-63b9-5e92-a7af-7fae4221c58d" "answer-d728cb75-8aff-5441-a02b-d2123ed28dfa" "answer-1059a7c8-9a04-5280-9f29-30f8d8a20897" "answer-e7761c70-8716-56c9-a2cb-a35290fdff3f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fb69761-90da-5ef3-9336-c74c1cd1883c" :answer/id #uuid "3fb69761-90da-5ef3-9336-c74c1cd1883c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3035fbac-63b9-5e92-a7af-7fae4221c58d" :answer/id #uuid "3035fbac-63b9-5e92-a7af-7fae4221c58d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d728cb75-8aff-5441-a02b-d2123ed28dfa" :answer/id #uuid "d728cb75-8aff-5441-a02b-d2123ed28dfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1059a7c8-9a04-5280-9f29-30f8d8a20897" :answer/id #uuid "1059a7c8-9a04-5280-9f29-30f8d8a20897" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7761c70-8716-56c9-a2cb-a35290fdff3f" :answer/id #uuid "e7761c70-8716-56c9-a2cb-a35290fdff3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116516 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the function\n\n$$\ny=5\\log_6(x-2).\n$$\n\nHow do we plot its graph?\n\nAs usual, we can do this by combining transformations of the curve $y=\\log_6 x.$\n\n- We start with the graph of $y=\\log_6 x{:}$\n\n![](images/a8/a85eb96e169e43568da76e33ee7f997af9196d26e867d7e7db1b2ddea0725d2a.png)\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=\\log_6{(x-2)}.$\n\n![](images/24/248a60c13ac422aeace5ba19f9f25716b188c19dbe04f5970287c587adb44c85.png)\n\nNotice that points $(1,0)$ and $(6,1)$ on the graph of $y=\\log_6 x$ map to the points $(3,0)$ and $(8,1)$ on the graph of $y=\\log_6 (x-2),$ respectively.\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5\\log_6{(x-2)}.$\n\n![](images/a6/a67b409068f67d81f0217aaeb30f76bd63c6501376d2adc567db7c4f319d7144.png)\n\nSo, to plot the curve of a transformed logarithmic function, we apply subsequent transformations presented in the given function, as usual." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115461 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119808 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c391d9c3-0c76-5e6f-b0c3-d6b4ee2c37cb" "question-a8427e64-b547-5ecb-a62e-824aa188b632" "question-ddfb229d-f01d-542c-8a6c-f2e9a5996154"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c391d9c3-0c76-5e6f-b0c3-d6b4ee2c37cb" :question/id #uuid "c391d9c3-0c76-5e6f-b0c3-d6b4ee2c37cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-105199" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7847d689-0e36-5ac4-b435-bd1cf0642bc0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7847d689-0e36-5ac4-b435-bd1cf0642bc0" :answer-field/id #uuid "7847d689-0e36-5ac4-b435-bd1cf0642bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-98c56786-6605-5401-b672-c63cb1cecd33" :answer-field/choices ["answer-976c158b-03de-5101-af86-c772a635e855" "answer-38fb4394-142f-596a-acba-652af141f7ea" "answer-1f1cbd99-3722-58a8-94e3-9b80df51c48b" "answer-1442cd36-01ab-57b5-b752-7b7131e887f9" "answer-98c56786-6605-5401-b672-c63cb1cecd33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-976c158b-03de-5101-af86-c772a635e855" :answer/id #uuid "976c158b-03de-5101-af86-c772a635e855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38fb4394-142f-596a-acba-652af141f7ea" :answer/id #uuid "38fb4394-142f-596a-acba-652af141f7ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f1cbd99-3722-58a8-94e3-9b80df51c48b" :answer/id #uuid "1f1cbd99-3722-58a8-94e3-9b80df51c48b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1442cd36-01ab-57b5-b752-7b7131e887f9" :answer/id #uuid "1442cd36-01ab-57b5-b752-7b7131e887f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98c56786-6605-5401-b672-c63cb1cecd33" :answer/id #uuid "98c56786-6605-5401-b672-c63cb1cecd33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8427e64-b547-5ecb-a62e-824aa188b632" :question/id #uuid "a8427e64-b547-5ecb-a62e-824aa188b632" :question/math-academy-id "q-34114" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-1)=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-283164aa-73de-5dcd-8a69-e6c9e6374abc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-283164aa-73de-5dcd-8a69-e6c9e6374abc" :answer-field/id #uuid "283164aa-73de-5dcd-8a69-e6c9e6374abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-35881d2a-16d1-5e15-a12f-b325e9f8db16" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d4a40a4-8887-524e-8b48-8a24128d12fd" "answer-35881d2a-16d1-5e15-a12f-b325e9f8db16" "answer-d639618d-a932-5b03-8225-fae8a0b21ef5" "answer-32966283-95f9-59c0-9da8-620246a44c9f" "answer-1e4a4287-0b4e-5134-b26d-e720107fbfce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d4a40a4-8887-524e-8b48-8a24128d12fd" :answer/id #uuid "4d4a40a4-8887-524e-8b48-8a24128d12fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35881d2a-16d1-5e15-a12f-b325e9f8db16" :answer/id #uuid "35881d2a-16d1-5e15-a12f-b325e9f8db16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d639618d-a932-5b03-8225-fae8a0b21ef5" :answer/id #uuid "d639618d-a932-5b03-8225-fae8a0b21ef5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32966283-95f9-59c0-9da8-620246a44c9f" :answer/id #uuid "32966283-95f9-59c0-9da8-620246a44c9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e4a4287-0b4e-5134-b26d-e720107fbfce" :answer/id #uuid "1e4a4287-0b4e-5134-b26d-e720107fbfce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ddfb229d-f01d-542c-8a6c-f2e9a5996154" :question/id #uuid "ddfb229d-f01d-542c-8a6c-f2e9a5996154" :question/math-academy-id "q-34639" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=18 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -8 & 18 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{3}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(3)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{a}_{1}+6{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 9\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=9\\cdot 6\\cdot 3=162.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d94e257-45da-5352-bd7b-f0680253dc5b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d94e257-45da-5352-bd7b-f0680253dc5b" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d94e257-45da-5352-bd7b-f0680253dc5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-012982fc-c0c6-5360-983d-5c24ecfa5a09" :answer-field/choices ["answer-012982fc-c0c6-5360-983d-5c24ecfa5a09" "answer-3ef56e44-9e80-5352-bfb2-5d35c8c19329" "answer-bcb4217f-2b11-5476-8d7f-3c0e1e75e42b" "answer-65e52e96-8639-5303-a9f5-06fb2d4d391c" "answer-84afc469-3802-566a-b03f-b05bcd3fd37e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-012982fc-c0c6-5360-983d-5c24ecfa5a09" :answer/id #uuid "012982fc-c0c6-5360-983d-5c24ecfa5a09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "162" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ef56e44-9e80-5352-bfb2-5d35c8c19329" :answer/id #uuid "3ef56e44-9e80-5352-bfb2-5d35c8c19329" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcb4217f-2b11-5476-8d7f-3c0e1e75e42b" :answer/id #uuid "bcb4217f-2b11-5476-8d7f-3c0e1e75e42b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65e52e96-8639-5303-a9f5-06fb2d4d391c" :answer/id #uuid "65e52e96-8639-5303-a9f5-06fb2d4d391c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84afc469-3802-566a-b03f-b05bcd3fd37e" :answer/id #uuid "84afc469-3802-566a-b03f-b05bcd3fd37e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119856 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-435bef4b-b3af-5768-973e-b4aa50ac129d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-435bef4b-b3af-5768-973e-b4aa50ac129d" :question/id #uuid "435bef4b-b3af-5768-973e-b4aa50ac129d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105235" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 10 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-6.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-6\\,⇒\\,k=6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a41c8e29-8919-560f-acd3-bb2401f756f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a41c8e29-8919-560f-acd3-bb2401f756f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "a41c8e29-8919-560f-acd3-bb2401f756f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e4b9832-4e75-5456-ae0f-c9cbef897a8d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05a3b4f0-37fc-510b-b782-70713c302e3e" "answer-79384773-3b33-5558-b1a4-01487217122d" "answer-d5b9c506-0e84-558b-99e5-7b80605f2397" "answer-d55768be-caa1-5015-a0a0-51da48688614" "answer-0e4b9832-4e75-5456-ae0f-c9cbef897a8d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05a3b4f0-37fc-510b-b782-70713c302e3e" :answer/id #uuid "05a3b4f0-37fc-510b-b782-70713c302e3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79384773-3b33-5558-b1a4-01487217122d" :answer/id #uuid "79384773-3b33-5558-b1a4-01487217122d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5b9c506-0e84-558b-99e5-7b80605f2397" :answer/id #uuid "d5b9c506-0e84-558b-99e5-7b80605f2397" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d55768be-caa1-5015-a0a0-51da48688614" :answer/id #uuid "d55768be-caa1-5015-a0a0-51da48688614" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e4b9832-4e75-5456-ae0f-c9cbef897a8d" :answer/id #uuid "0e4b9832-4e75-5456-ae0f-c9cbef897a8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120304 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc,$ $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨1,5,8⟩$, $c=⟨-1,-1,-3⟩$, and  $u=⟨-3,5,-7⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s-t=-3 \\\\ 5s-t=5 \\\\ 8s-3t=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 8 & -3 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 8 & -3 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-8{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-8,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $u$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117963 :answer-field/correct 17592186117964 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121035 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1c9dbcb8-61c8-5cf9-96d4-e96bf0a4fe24" :activity/id #uuid "1c9dbcb8-61c8-5cf9-96d4-e96bf0a4fe24" :activity/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048131 :activity/course 17592186046668 :activity/steps ["step-0e849385-5c84-5ebb-9c6a-2fbbf98e75fc" "step-9183cf7f-7ba1-5cfc-a202-00bfee4b32c5" "step-86d18b44-9b63-5aac-a2f2-3778afdcb862" "step-40081e28-38bb-5e80-8b2b-ef32997c6f47" "step-2eae129d-e797-5673-86cf-a806611e2fca"] :activity/first-step "step-0e849385-5c84-5ebb-9c6a-2fbbf98e75fc" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-0e849385-5c84-5ebb-9c6a-2fbbf98e75fc" :step/id #uuid "0e849385-5c84-5ebb-9c6a-2fbbf98e75fc" :step/content 17592186362318 :step/next "step-9183cf7f-7ba1-5cfc-a202-00bfee4b32c5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9183cf7f-7ba1-5cfc-a202-00bfee4b32c5" :step/id #uuid "9183cf7f-7ba1-5cfc-a202-00bfee4b32c5" :step/content 17592186362323 :step/next "step-86d18b44-9b63-5aac-a2f2-3778afdcb862" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-86d18b44-9b63-5aac-a2f2-3778afdcb862" :step/id #uuid "86d18b44-9b63-5aac-a2f2-3778afdcb862" :step/content 17592186395542 :step/next "step-40081e28-38bb-5e80-8b2b-ef32997c6f47" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-40081e28-38bb-5e80-8b2b-ef32997c6f47" :step/id #uuid "40081e28-38bb-5e80-8b2b-ef32997c6f47" :step/content 17592186374986 :step/next "step-2eae129d-e797-5673-86cf-a806611e2fca" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2eae129d-e797-5673-86cf-a806611e2fca" :step/id #uuid "2eae129d-e797-5673-86cf-a806611e2fca" :step/content 17592186314938 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184320 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-60bf46ec-c41d-5bd2-93b6-b056ca651773" "question-bd6c8038-fcc2-5fa5-ad44-54f20efc3514"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-60bf46ec-c41d-5bd2-93b6-b056ca651773" :question/id #uuid "60bf46ec-c41d-5bd2-93b6-b056ca651773" :question/math-academy-id "q-3077" :question/problem "$2\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9b}=$" :question/worked-solution "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{9b} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{8a{b}^{3}\\cdot 9b} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{72a{b}^{4}} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{36{b}^{4}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{(6{b}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{(6{b}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |6{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot |6|\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot 6\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 6\\cdot |{b}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 36\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a8b84a5-9da7-5fc7-9d58-31f7e5a5ff16"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a8b84a5-9da7-5fc7-9d58-31f7e5a5ff16" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a8b84a5-9da7-5fc7-9d58-31f7e5a5ff16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9a2ab45f-3b8e-59d8-ae0d-a84bcaf91d90" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc68b7fb-a728-5929-84b3-caef82248a8a" "answer-4ee323e3-08d6-546b-8935-72052398ce78" "answer-9a2ab45f-3b8e-59d8-ae0d-a84bcaf91d90" "answer-02d5136f-8e3c-524a-ae1b-d2c7d6281429" "answer-cfba2d73-242a-5b18-a815-bc4b2611b506"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc68b7fb-a728-5929-84b3-caef82248a8a" :answer/id #uuid "fc68b7fb-a728-5929-84b3-caef82248a8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18{b}^{2}\\sqrt{a}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ee323e3-08d6-546b-8935-72052398ce78" :answer/id #uuid "4ee323e3-08d6-546b-8935-72052398ce78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36a{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a2ab45f-3b8e-59d8-ae0d-a84bcaf91d90" :answer/id #uuid "9a2ab45f-3b8e-59d8-ae0d-a84bcaf91d90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{2a}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02d5136f-8e3c-524a-ae1b-d2c7d6281429" :answer/id #uuid "02d5136f-8e3c-524a-ae1b-d2c7d6281429" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36{b}^{2}\\sqrt{a}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfba2d73-242a-5b18-a815-bc4b2611b506" :answer/id #uuid "cfba2d73-242a-5b18-a815-bc4b2611b506" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6a{b}^{2}\\sqrt{11a}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bd6c8038-fcc2-5fa5-ad44-54f20efc3514" :question/id #uuid "bd6c8038-fcc2-5fa5-ad44-54f20efc3514" :question/math-academy-id "q-141498" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b}\\cdot \\sqrt{15abc}$ given that $a,b,c\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We perform the multiplication under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b}\\cdot \\sqrt{15abc} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6{a}^{3}b\\cdot 15abc} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{90{a}^{4}{b}^{2}c} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{a}^{4}{b}^{2}\\cdot 10c} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3{a}^{2}b)^{2}\\cdot 10c} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\sqrt{10c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have that $|{a}^{2}|={a}^{2}$ and $|b|=b.$  Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |{a}^{2}|\\cdot |b|\\cdot \\sqrt{10c} & = \\\\ 3{a}^{2}b\\sqrt{10c} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-19a40071-b4ac-5acd-960c-d9a0e6445961"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19a40071-b4ac-5acd-960c-d9a0e6445961" :answer-field/id #uuid "19a40071-b4ac-5acd-960c-d9a0e6445961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4d77eb1e-d305-5769-be60-4814a62a8015" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20037864-692e-5bfb-9322-3b5120acbe5c" "answer-4d77eb1e-d305-5769-be60-4814a62a8015" "answer-d988c7ff-7f4c-5175-9b2b-46df6a682046" "answer-85b799e3-d57c-539f-acb8-811ec4f3f811" "answer-e3bd976f-4efd-55f3-be7f-8f74b45ff7e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:d